From 9d8c201a13dc26d4b7747468025ced4b1c9c9e5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josh Vanderberg <jvanderberg@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:35:54 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] Add an armv7m target (ARMv7-M Thumb-2, soft-float EABI)

arm-thumb-gen.c is a code generator for ARMv7-M (the Cortex-M3/M4/M33
instruction set): Thumb-2 only, since these cores have no ARM state,
and the soft-float EABI: float values live in one core register,
double (= long double) and long long values in a pair, and every
floating point operation is a call to an EABI run-time helper
(__aeabi_dadd & co, in libgcc). Integer division is SDIV/UDIV. The
calling convention, parameter assignment and frame handling come from
arm-gen.c (which emits ARM code); the soft-float support follows
riscv32-gen.c. Calls are BL with R_ARM_THM_CALL relocations, addresses
movw/movt pairs (no literal pools), and functions get bit 0 set in
their symbol value and a $t mapping symbol.

It is TCC_TARGET_ARM with TCC_ARM_EABI and TCC_ARM_THUMB: cross target
"armv7m" (make armv7m-tcc), configure --cpu=armv7m for a native one.
Programs are static (there is no dynamic linker) and linked with
libgcc.a. There is no Thumb-2 assembler: asm statements and .S files
are not supported; lib/atomic-armv7m.c and the Thumb alloca in
lib/alloca.S are for a gcc-built libtcc1.a. wchar_t is unsigned int,
as the ARM ABI has it.
